ata-file-width=3D"5621" data-file-height=3D"8231"></a></span>
</td>
<td style=3D"padding:0 6px 0 0">
<p><i><b><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Three_Beauties_of_the_Pre=
sent_Day" title=3D"Three Beauties of the Present Day">Three Beauties of the=
 Present Day</a></b></i> is a <i class=3D"nowrap" lang=3D"ja-Latn"><a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nishiki-e" title=3D"Nishiki-e">nishiki-e<=
/a></i> colour woodblock print produced circa 1792=E2=80=9393 by the Japane=
se <span class=3D"nowrap" lang=3D"ja-Latn"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.=
org/wiki/Ukiyo-e" title=3D"Ukiyo-e">ukiyo-e</a></span> artist <a href=3D"ht=
tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Utamaro" title=3D"Utamaro">Kitagawa Utamaro</a>=
. The triangular composition depicts the busts of three celebrity beauties =
of the time: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geisha" title=3D"Geis=
ha">geisha</a> Tomimoto Toyohina (middle), and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/wiki/Ochaya" title=3D"Ochaya">teahouse</a> waitresses Takashima His=
a (left) and Naniwa Kita (right), each adorned with an identifying family c=
rest (<i lang=3D"ja-Latn"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mon_(emb=
lem)" title=3D"Mon (emblem)">mon</a></i>). Subtle differences can be detect=
ed in the subjects' faces&nbsp;=E2=80=93 a level of individualized realism =
at the time unusual in ukiyo-e, and a contrast with the stereotyped beautie=
s in earlier masters such as <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suzuk=
i_Harunobu" title=3D"Suzuki Harunobu">Harunobu</a> and <a href=3D"https://e=
n.wikipedia.org/wiki/Torii_Kiyonaga" title=3D"Torii Kiyonaga">Kiyonaga</a>.=
 The triangular positioning became a vogue in the 1790s. Utamaro produced s=
everal other pictures with this arrangement of the same three beauties, and=
 each appeared in numerous other portraits by Utamaro and other artists. Ut=
amaro was the leading ukiyo-e artist in the 1790s in the <i lang=3D"ja-Latn=
"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bijin-ga" title=3D"Bijin-ga">bij=
in-ga</a></i> genre of pictures of female beauties, and was known in partic=
ular for his <i lang=3D"ja-Latn"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%=
C5%8Ckubi-e" title=3D"=C5=8Ckubi-e">=C5=8Dkubi-e</a></i> prints, a style of=
 <span class=3D"nowrap" lang=3D"ja-Latn">ukiyo-e</span> that focuses on the=
 heads. The luxurious print was published by <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Tsutaya_J%C5%ABzabur%C5%8D" title=3D"Tsutaya J=C5=ABzabur=C5=8D"=
>Tsutaya J=C5=ABzabur=C5=8D</a> and made with multiple woodblocks=E2=80=94o=
ne for each colour=E2=80=94and the background was dusted with <a href=3D"ht=
tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muscovite" title=3D"Muscovite">muscovite</a> to=
 produce a glimmering effect. This copy of <i>Three Beauties of the Present=
 Day</i> is in the collection of the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi=
ki/Toledo_Museum_of_Art" title=3D"Toledo Museum of Art">Toledo Museum of Ar=
t</a> in Ohio.
</p>
